## Selection rule

| SEL at mux | State | OUT follows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1000 µs at 50 Hz (valid, below threshold) | not active | **Pi M1/M2** |
| 2000 µs at 50 Hz (valid, above threshold) | active | **Alta S1/S2 (neutral only)** |
| no valid pulse, FAILMODE OFF | invalid | **Pi M1/M2** |
| no valid pulse, FAILMODE ON | invalid | outputs low — **no pulse, NOT neutral** |
| mux unpowered | — | **no signal — NOT neutral** |

Manufacturer defaults: threshold 1696 µs, ±64 µs hysteresis, three good pulses to qualify, ≈130 ms
of no activity before SEL is invalid. The mux has **no safe-start**.

## Deliberate design rules

1. **Only the Alta writes SEL.** The Pi never connects to SEL, so no two GPIOs are tied.
2. **Alta S1/S2 carry neutral and nothing else**, from boot through takeover. That is the only reason
   a frozen valid slave-select pulse is tolerable: the slave inputs never hold a driving angle.
3. **Servo power never passes through the mux.** OUT pins carry signal only; each servo is fed from
   its own converter through its own protection.
4. **Mux logic must survive either host.** It is not powered from a Pi or Alta header rail, because
   losing the host that powers the mux would remove *both* steering paths.
